      "sample_index": 2,
      "text": "Let's start by visualizing the traveler's journey on a coordinate plane. We can place the starting point at the origin, (0, 0), and then move the traveler in each leg of the journey.\n\n1. First, the traveler heads 18 miles north, so we move up by 18 units: (0, 0) → (0, 18).\n2. Then, the traveler heads 11 miles west, so we move left by 11 units: (0, 18) → (11, 18).\n3. Next, the traveler heads 6 miles south, so we move down by 6 units: (11, 18) → (11, 12).\n4. Finally, the traveler heads 6 miles east, so we move right by 6 units: (11, 12) → (17, 12).\n\nNow, we can calculate the distance from the starting point to the final location using the distance formula:\n\\[ \\text{Distance} = \\sqrt{(x_2 - x_1)^2 + (y_2 - y_1)^2} \\]\nwhere \\((x_1, y_1)\\) is the starting point and \\((x_2, y_2)\\) is the final location.\n\nPlugging in the values:\n\\[ \\text{Distance} = \\sqrt{(17 - 0)^2 + (12 - 0)^2} = \\sqrt{17^2 + 12^2} \\]\n\\[ \\text{Distance} = \\sqrt{289 + 144} = \\sqrt{433} \\]\n\nThe final answer is \\(\\boxed{\\sqrt{433}}\\) miles from the starting point.",
