      "text": "To solve this problem, we need to determine the net displacement of the traveler in both the north-south and east-west directions.\n\nStep 1: Calculate the net displacement in the north-south direction.\nThe traveler initially heads 18 miles north, then 6 miles south. The net displacement in the north-south direction is:\n18 miles - 6 miles = 12 miles north.\n\nStep 2: Calculate the net displacement in the east-west direction.\nThe traveler initially heads 11 miles west, then 6 miles east. The net displacement in the east-west direction is:\n11 miles - 6 miles = 5 miles west.\n\nStep 3: Calculate the total distance traveled in each direction.\nThe total distance traveled in the north-south direction is 12 miles, and the total distance traveled in the east-west direction is 5 miles.\n\nStep 4: Calculate the final distance from the starting point.\nTo find the final distance from the starting point, we need to use the Pythagorean theorem. The final distance is the hypotenuse of a right triangle with legs of 12 miles (north-south direction) and 5 miles (east-west direction). The formula for the Pythagorean theorem is:\n\\[ c = \\sqrt{a^2 + b^2} \\]\nwhere \\( c \\) is the final distance, \\( a \\) is the north-south distance, and \\( b \\) is the east-west distance.\n\nSubstituting the given values into the formula:\n\\[ c = \\sqrt{12^2 + 5^2} \\]\n\\[ c = \\sqrt{144 + 25} \\]\n\\[ c = \\sqrt{169} \\]\n\\[ c = 13 \\]\n\nTherefore, the traveler is \\(\\boxed{13}\\) miles from the starting point after these four legs of the journey.",
